values ('b4c5d6e7-8f9a-4b22-9c12-2d3e4f5a6b42', 'political-philosophy', 'Political Philosophy', 'Students explain major theories of authority, legitimacy, liberty, equality, rights, democracy, punishment, property, and global justice, reconstruct and evaluate arguments from classical, modern, and contemporary political philosophy, and compare liberal, libertarian, republican, socialist, feminist, critical, and multicultural approaches to political life. They apply normative concepts to law, public policy, institutions, human rights, migration, war, climate, and democratic practice, use evidence and institutional context without confusing empirical description with normative justification, and produce a policy-focused philosophical argument.');

